WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.391
Environment variables should usually be read and validated during
application startup, before the application begins accepting work.

2
00:00:07.391 --> 00:00:15.801
Startup validation lets the service fail early when a required value is
missing, malformed, out of range, or inconsistent with the deployment
environment.

3
00:00:15.801 --> 00:00:22.618
This is much safer than discovering the problem halfway through a request,
background job, or security-sensitive operation.

4
00:00:22.618 --> 00:00:33.004
Validation should include required variables, optional variables, default
values, type conversion, boolean parsing, numeric limits, allowed values,
and relationships between settings.

5
00:00:33.004 --> 00:00:36.699
A boolean should not become true merely because a string is nonempty.

6
00:00:36.699 --> 00:00:39.120
A timeout should not accept a negative value.

7
00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:42.433
A deployment environment should be one of the approved names.

8
00:00:42.433 --> 00:00:47.021
A security-critical setting should not quietly fall back to a development
behavior.

9
00:00:47.021 --> 00:00:50.907
Configuration schema concepts help teams make validation consistent.

10
00:00:50.907 --> 00:00:58.489
A schema can describe each variable, its purpose, type, sensitivity,
allowed values, default behavior, owner, and whether it can be logged.

11
00:00:58.489 --> 00:01:04.542
Even a simple internal convention is better than scattered reads from the
environment throughout the codebase.

12
00:01:04.542 --> 00:01:09.321
Centralized loading makes review easier and reduces accidental drift
between modules.

13
00:01:09.321 --> 00:01:17.986
Fail-fast behavior is especially important for authentication,
authorization, TLS validation, logging, encryption, token validation, and
debug behavior.

14
00:01:17.986 --> 00:01:25.185
If a required key or endpoint is missing, the application should not
silently disable protection or switch to a broad permissive mode.

15
00:01:25.185 --> 00:01:28.180
Safe defaults should preserve security expectations.

16
00:01:28.180 --> 00:01:34.360
Missing or invalid configuration should produce controlled operational
errors, not weakened runtime behavior.
